    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(numRows: number)`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Row r always has r + 1 entries and both of its ends are 1, so only the entries strictly inside need to be computed.",
    "You do not need binomial coefficients. Each inner entry equals the sum of the two entries directly above it in the row you already built, which is one addition per entry.",
    "Keep one working row and copy it into the answer before you overwrite it, otherwise every stored row will alias the same growing list."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force treats every cell as an independent binomial coefficient and recomputes it with a loop of k multiplications and divisions, which makes row r cost O(r\u00b2) and the whole triangle O(numRows\u00b3). The optimal version reuses the row above: each inner entry of the new row is one addition of two neighbouring entries from the previous row, so the triangle is produced in O(numRows\u00b2) time with O(numRows) extra space for the working row.",
    "why_optimal": "The answer itself contains 1 + 2 + ... + numRows entries, which is O(numRows\u00b2), so no algorithm can beat quadratic time. The row recurrence produces each entry with a single addition and never recomputes a coefficient it already knows, so the quadratic bound is met and only the previous row has to be kept in memory.",
    "pitfalls": "Row r holds r + 1 entries, so an inner loop that runs to r - 1 loses the last entry and a loop that runs to r copies the value twice. Pushing the working row into the result without copying it means later updates mutate rows already stored, and every returned row turns out identical. The multiplicative binomial formula must divide by i + 1 at each step and not at the end, otherwise truncation destroys the value, and the working row has to start at [1] so that row 0 is right."
  },
